+# Background Processes
+
+Managed long-lived processes for pi: start them, page their logs, stop the whole tree, and let a process wake the agent instead of the agent polling.
+
+```bash
+# Quick test
+pi -e packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/background-processes
+
+# Auto-discovery (subdirectory with index.ts)
+mkdir -p ~/.pi/agent/extensions/background-processes
+ln -sf "$(pwd)/packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/background-processes/index.ts" \
+  ~/.pi/agent/extensions/background-processes/index.ts
+ln -sf "$(pwd)/packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/background-processes/manager.ts" \
+  ~/.pi/agent/extensions/background-processes/manager.ts
+```
+
+`DefaultResourceLoader` loads the same `index.ts` layout from `~/.pi/agent/extensions/*/index.ts` or `.pi/extensions/*/index.ts`.
+
+## Tools
+
+Every tool returns a JSON object as the result text and attaches the same object as `details`. The exception is `bash` on its normal (non-backgrounded) path, which keeps the built-in result text, truncation, and error strings.
+
+### `bg_run`
+
+`{ command: string; cwd?: string; name?: string; wake?: { ready?: string; error?: string }; cleanup?: { command: string; timeoutSec?: number } }`
+
+Starts `command` through the configured shell and returns immediately with a process record, including a fresh `id`. Relative `cwd` resolves against the session working directory; the record stores the absolute path.
+
+`wake.ready` and `wake.error` are regular expressions matched against each output line.
+
+`cleanup.command` runs after `bg_kill` has torn down the process tree, in the same working directory, bounded by `cleanup.timeoutSec` (default 30).
+
+### `bg_logs`
+
+`{ id: string; offset?: number; limit?: number }` → `{ id, offset, lines, total }`
+
+A page of combined stdout/stderr lines, the line offset actually served, and the total line count so far. A page never exceeds 64 KB. If `limit` is omitted, the newest lines that fit are returned. Output is stored on disk so pi's heap does not grow with log volume.
+
+### `bg_list`
+
+`{}` → `{ processes }`
+
+Every process record visible in this pi process, running and finished.
+
+### `bg_kill`
+
+`{ id: string; timeoutSec?: number }`
+
+Stops the process and all of its descendants: `SIGTERM` first, then `SIGKILL` after `timeoutSec` (default 5). If the process was started with `cleanup`, that command runs once the tree is gone. Returns the final record plus `cleanup: { ran, exitCode, failed } | null`. A failed or timed-out cleanup is `failed: true`.
+
+### `bg_watch`
+
+`{ id: string; ready?: string | null; error?: string | null; exit?: boolean; output?: boolean }`
+
+Changes wake rules of a running process and returns its record. `null` removes a pattern. `exit: false` silences the exit wake. `output: false` silences the auto-background output wake.
+
+### `bash`
+
+`{ command: string; timeout?: number; stalledSec?: number }`
+
+Runs in the foreground like the built-in tool until one of:
+
+- It finishes (exit, `timeout`, or abort) before going silent for `stalledSec` seconds (default 30). Returns the same result text, truncation, and tool-error strings as the built-in tool. A `timeout` shorter than `stalledSec` wins. No process record is created.
+- It produces no stdout/stderr bytes for `stalledSec` seconds while still running. The tool returns within one second of that threshold with the process record as JSON text and `details`, including `backgrounded: true` and the `stalledSec` that applied. Output printed before the switch is the head of the log. From then on the process is a normal managed process.
+
+## Process record
+
+At least: `id`, `name`, `command`, `cwd`, `pid`, `state` (`running` | `exited` | `killed`), `exitCode` (number or `null`), `startedAt` / `endedAt` (ISO-8601; `endedAt` is `null` while running), `logLines`, `backgrounded` (`true` only when `bash` moved the command to the background).
+
+## How a process wakes the agent
+
+Custom message `customType: "background-process"`. `details`: `{ id, name, reasons, exitCode, matchedLine }` where `reasons` is a non-empty array drawn from `"ready" | "error" | "output" | "exit"`, `exitCode` is a number or `null`, and `matchedLine` is the first triggering line or `null`.
+
+- By default only process exit wakes the agent.
+- `ready` and `error` wake only when the corresponding pattern was supplied.
+- `output` applies only to a command that `bash` moved to the background: the first complete output line after the switch, with that line as `matchedLine`.
+- `ready` and `output` wake at most once per process.
+- `error` wakes at most once per delivery window.
+- `exit` wakes exactly once when the process leaves `running`, including `bg_kill` (`state: "killed"`).
+- Nothing wakes after the exit wake has been delivered, and nothing wakes for a reason `bg_watch` silenced.
+
+While the agent is busy, the wake is delivered after the current assistant turn has finished its tool calls and before the next model request (`triggerTurn` + steer). While idle, it starts a new turn immediately.
+
+Several reasons for the same process in one window may be combined into one message. Several processes in the same window are reported in the order each first fired.
+
+The wake goes to the session that started the process if that session is still active in this pi process; otherwise to the current active session.
+
+## Lifecycle
+
+- Processes belong to the running pi process, not to a session. `/reload`, `/new`, `/fork`, and tree navigation do not stop them. Any session in the same pi process can list, read, and kill them.
+- On pi exit (normal, `SIGTERM`, or `SIGINT`) every managed process is stopped with `SIGTERM` then `SIGKILL`, including children the command spawned. No managed process outlives pi. Those signals still terminate pi after children are stopped.
+- Logs and final records are stored under `{sessionDir}/background-processes/`. A later pi process that resumes the session can `bg_list` finished records and `bg_logs` their output; those records are `exited` or `killed`, never `running`.
